<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastern Orthodox Church</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Orthodox Church, Orthodox Christianity, Orthodox</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eastern Orthodox Church is the body of Christian churches that follow the Byzantine tradition and recognise the authority of the ecumenical councils, separating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. It was the state church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and it remained the faith of many subjects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and of the Commonwealth's eastern population. The site also links it to the Christian traditions of the Aksumite and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empires</a>, whose church belongs to the related Oriental Orthodox communion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church.jpg" alt="Eastern Orthodox Church" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Klearchos Kapoutsis from Santorini, Greece · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_St._George,_Istanbul_(August_2010).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Roman Catholicism was the dynasty's faith and the established church, although Protestants, <b>Orthodox Christians</b> and Jews were tolerated under the reforms of Joseph II.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Byzantine government combined the Roman tradition of codified law and professional administration with a distinctively Christian ideology that made the emperor both God's chosen representative on earth and the patron and protector of the <b>Orthodox Church</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Khmelnytsky's multi-vectored diplomacy, weighing an <b>Orthodox</b>–Protestant bloc, a third partner's place in the Commonwealth or the Ottoman orbit, drew the Tsardom, Poland, Sweden, Transylvania and the Porte into the Ukrainian question.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The southern coast of the peninsula was then held by the Genoese colony of Caffa and the <b>Orthodox</b> principality of Theodoro.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Orthodox</b> monasteries such as Sumela, Vazelon and Peristereota held large estates in the mountains, and the Church of Saint Eugenios was the centre of civic religion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ethiopia's seven-century survival as an independent Christian kingdom surrounded by Islamic states shaped the religious and ethnic geography of the entire Horn of Africa, with the <b>Orthodox Church</b> providing institutional continuity across periods when the imperial state had effectively ceased to function.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
